result:
{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q009_heme_bundle_fold_not_homology","answer":"No. SCOPe does not support treating these three groups as one homologous superfamily. In SCOPe 2.08 (the current stable release, which I used for all queries), they share the fold f.21 'Heme-binding four-helical bundle' (sunid 81344) but are three separate superfamilies: f.21.1 'Transmembrane di-heme cytochromes' (sunid 81342), f.21.2 'Fumarate reductase respiratory complex transmembrane subunits' (sunid 81343), and f.21.3 'Respiratory nitrate reductase 1 gamma chain' (sunid 103501). The shared level is the fold, which supports only structural similarity (the fold annotation describes four transmembrane helices in an up-and-down bundle with one or two hemes bound between the helices). The fold's homology warning states that SCOPe levels above superfamily do not imply homology. Because the three superfamilies are distinct, SCOPe makes no positive assertion of common ancestry at the superfamily level. That is not a proof that they are unrelated, analogous, or convergent; SCOPe does not establish that negative claim, and any such claim would need evidence outside hierarchy placement, which I did not retrieve.

The superfamily-level annotation for f.21.1 says three of the four heme ligands are conserved between the two families and that both heme groups bind similarly but not identically. The response does not say which families this refers to, so I report it as written. It describes heme-site geometry only; it does not establish a substrate, reaction, or transferable function. The f.21.3 annotation notes a possible link to the other two superfamilies: its subunit corresponds to the gamma subunit of formate dehydrogenase N but is structurally closer to fumarate reductase FrdC. This is a curator note about a possible relationship, not a SCOPe merger or homology assertion, and it would need sequence or structure alignment evidence before being used as a homology claim. The f.21.2 annotation notes two distinct families, one with the fold in a single-chain subunit and one with it formed by two chains.

Limits: a four-helical bundle with heme is not by itself a SCOPe criterion. Cytochrome c' (a.24.3.2, e.g., 1cgn) also has a four-helical heme bundle but sits in a different fold, a.24. The search for 'fumarate reductase' and 'nitrate reductase' returned no results, which does not establish absence. I mapped the prompt's named proteins to f.21.2 and f.21.3 using the superfamily descriptions. I did not retrieve member domains of f.21.2 or f.21.3, so I have not verified that any specific named protein belongs to them. No alignment or structural residue-correspondence evidence was retrieved, so no homology claim across these superfamilies is supported here.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","GET /api/v1/releases/current","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=fumarate%20reductase&limit=10","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=nitrate%20reductase%20gamma&limit=10","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=cytochrome%20b558&limit=10","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=cytochrome%20b6&limit=10","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/children?limit=100","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81342/children?limit=100","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/homology","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/annotations","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81342/homology","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81342/annotations","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81343","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81343/parents","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81343/annotations","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103501","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103501/parents","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103501/annotations","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103498/parents","GET /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/16548/parents"]}]}
